The key point is that one cannot associate the LC order parameter \(\Phi\) to the imaginary part of the CDW order parameter \(W\). This becomes even clearer for time-reversal invariant momenta \((-\mathbf{Q}=\mathbf{Q})\). Since this is the case for the \(A\)V\({}_{3}\)Sb\({}_{5}\) kagome metals, it is instructive to discuss it in more depth [15; 16; 17]. The space group of these materials is P6/mmm, which is the "most symmetric" hexagonal space group. Consider a CDW wave-vector located at the M point of the Brillouin zone, illustrated in Fig.1(c) (in these materials, the CDW wave-vector is actually at the L point, which is displaced from the M point along the \(q_{z}\) axis by \(Q_{z}=1/2\)). Because there are three M\({}_{i}\) points related by three-fold rotations, the CDW order parameter has three components, \(W=(W_{1},\,W_{2},\,W_{3})\), with each \(W_{i}\) associated with the Fourier component of the charge density at the corresponding wave-vector M\({}_{i}\). Since M\({}_{i}\) are time-reversal invariant-momenta, \(W_{i}\) are real-valued. Moreover, as explained in the caption of Fig. 1, group theory says that these three-component order parameters must transform as one of eight space-group irreps denoted \(M_{a}^{\pm}\) (with \(a=1,2,3,4\)). The triple-\(\mathbf{Q}\) configuration of CDW bond order \(W=(W_{0},W_{0},W_{0})\), as well as a CDW site order transforming as the same irrep \(M_{1}^{+}\), are shown in Fig. 1(a).

There are also eight possible three-component LC order parameters \(\Phi=(\Phi_{1},\,\Phi_{2},\,\Phi_{3})\), which transform as the irreps \(mM_{i}^{\pm}\) and break TR symmetry. The triple-\(\mathbf{Q}\) LC configuration with \(\Phi=(\phi_{0},\phi_{0},\phi_{0})\) transforming as the irrep \(mM_{2}^{+}\) is shown in Fig. 1(b). In Fig. 1(d), a single-\(\mathbf{Q}\) loop-current configuration is shown together with an SDW with the same symmetry properties. Note that different loop-current configurations may transform as the same irrep [18]. Thus, while it can be theoretically insightful to associate \(\Phi\) and \(W\) as real and imaginary parts of a composite complex order parameter that transforms as a reducible representation [19; 20], it is important to keep in mind that a hypothetical degeneracy between the two components is necessarily lifted by the crystal symmetries [20].

A crucial aspect of the problem is that the three-fold rotational symmetry of the kagome lattice allows anharmonic couplings between the CDW and LC order parameters, manifested as third-order terms in the coupled free energy of \(W\) and \(\Phi\)[16; 20]. The main consequence of this coupling is that the condensation of an LC order parameter with components at two distinct wave-vectors M\({}_{i_{1}}\) and M\({}_{i_{2}}\) must trigger a CDW order-parameter component at the third wave-vector M\({}_{i_{3}}=\) M\({}_{i_{2}}+\) M\({}_{i_{2}}\), i.e., \(W_{i_{1}}\sim\Phi_{i_{2}}\Phi_{i_{3}}\) (with \(i_{1}\), \(i_{2}\), and \(i_{3}\) all different from each other). In other words, a modulation of the phases of the hopping parameters typically induces a modulation in their amplitudes.

As a result of this non-trivial coupling, the phase diagram of the M-point coupled LC-CDW orders on hexagonal crystals can be very rich. While the many independent coefficients of the Landau free energy render it challenging to make generic statements about the stable phases, a few flux-CDW states stand out [16; 17; 20], as shown in Table 1 and Fig. 2. The only phase that allows for LC order and no CDW order is the single-\(\mathbf{Q}\) LC configuration \(\Phi=(\Phi_{0},0,0)\) (and, of course, its symmetry-equivalent domains). In contrast, the multi-\(\mathbf{Q}\) LC phases will necessarily be accompanied by CDW order.

