Fig. 2(a) (top row of Table 1), the magnetic moments generated by the loop currents do not cancel each other exactly, resulting in a net ferromagnetic moment. The anharmonic coupling between the LC and CDW order parameters is essential for this result, as the bond distortions generated by the loop currents make the local environment experienced by the currents inequivalent [16]. On the other hand, as shown in Fig. 2(b) (middle row of Table 1), in the 2**Q**-1**Q** configuration described by \(\Phi=(\Phi_{0},\Phi_{0},0)\) and \(W=(0,0,W_{0})\), the magnetic moments generated by the loop currents cancel. This configuration has the symmetry of an antiferromagnet, in that it possess magnetic translation symmetry. In contrast to the 3**Q**-3**Q** state, however, it breaks the six-fold rotational symmetry of the kagome lattice. Another interesting phase, displayed in Fig. 2(c) (bottom row of Table 1), is the 2**Q**-3**Q** state described by \(\Phi=(\Phi_{0},0,-\Phi_{0})\) and \(W=\left(W_{0},\tilde{W}_{0},W_{0}\right)\), dubbed the congruent CDW flux phase [21]. In this case, the loop-current fluxes cancel, implying a vanishing net magnetic dipole moment, but the system still has a net magnetic octupole moment, which gives rise to piezomagnetism.

_Microscopic Mechanisms_. The group-theory analysis provides important insights into the properties of the LC order, but it cannot establish which microscopic mechanisms promote this electronic instability. This requires either low-energy microscopic models of interacting electrons, or _ab initio_ calculations on materials.

Different models have been proposed to capture the M-point LC order relevant to the kagome metals, most of which have focused on the interplay between van Hove singularities, longer-range Coulomb repulsion, and the multi-orbital or multi-sublattice character of these systems [22; 23; 24; 25; 26; 27; 28; 29; 30]. As an illustration, we consider the patch model, which focuses on the low-energy electronic states around the M points [19; 20]. The motivation for this model is that simple tight-binding models on hexagonal lattices [19] exhibit electronic dispersions \(E(\mathbf{k})\) with saddle points at the M points, as shown in Fig. 3(a) for the kagome lattice

\[E\left(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{\mathrm{M}}\right)\approx E_{0}+\frac{\hbar^{2} \left(k_{x}^{2}-k_{y}^{2}\right)}{2m^{*}}, \tag{1}\]

where \(k_{x}\) and \(k_{y}\) point along the \(\Gamma\)-M and M-K directions, respectively, and \(m^{*}\) is the effective mass. In two dimensions, the density of states (DOS) displays a logarithmic divergence at \(E_{0}\) (Fig. 3(a)), whereas in three dimensions it displays a kink. These are known as van Hove singularities (vHs), which dominate the low-energy behavior if the chemical potential is close to \(E_{0}\).

The patch model defines three "flavors" of electronic states, one for each M point, as well as four symmetry allowed interactions \(g_{i}\) between them, as seen in Fig. 3(b). Because of the divergent DOS, weak-coupling approaches, such as the renormalization group, can be employed. The results (details in Box 2) reveal that, depending on which of the four interactions are dominant, the resulting instability of the model is towards a LC state.

Since most microscopic models rely on vHs, it is important to discuss the band structure of \(A\)V\({}_{3}\)Sb\({}_{5}\). In these materials, the vHs originate from the V \(d\)-orbitals [11; 31]. The saddle points are close to the Fermi level on the \(g_{z}=0\) plane, and there is sufficient dispersion along

